## Testing & Validation

### Testing Pyramid

```
       /\
      /E2E\      ← Few, slow, expensive (real APIs)
     /------\
    /Integr-\   ← Some, moderate speed
   /----------\
  /   Unit     \ ← Many, fast, isolated
 /--------------\
```

**Target Distribution:**
- 70% Unit tests (fast, isolated, mocked)
- 20% Integration tests (moderate, some real components)
- 10% E2E tests (slow, expensive, real APIs)

### Testing Standards

**Coverage Requirements:**
- Minimum 90% code coverage for production code
- 100% coverage for critical paths
- Test both success AND failure paths
- Include edge cases and boundary conditions

**Test Organization:**
- `tests/` directory mirrors `src/` structure
- One test file per source file (`test_module.py` for `module.py`)
- Group related tests in classes
- Use descriptive test names (`test_should_x_when_y`)

**Test Types:**

1. **Unit Tests**
   - Test individual functions/methods in isolation
   - Mock all external dependencies (APIs, DB, file system)
   - Fast (< 1 second per test)
   - Focus on logic and edge cases

2. **Integration Tests**
   - Test multiple components working together
   - Minimize mocking (test real interactions)
   - Use test databases/fixtures
   - Validate data flow between components

3. **End-to-End Tests**
   - Test complete workflows with real APIs
   - Use checkpoint/resume for expensive operations
   - Generate golden datasets for reproducibility
   - Run on CI before releases

4. **Performance Tests**
   - Establish baseline benchmarks
   - Set acceptable thresholds
   - Track metrics over time
   - Fail on regressions

5. **Regression Tests**
   - Test for previously fixed bugs
   - Ensure fixes stay fixed
   - Document the original issue
   - Include reproduction steps

### Validation Best Practices (from Industry)

**1. Clear, Measurable Requirements**
- Use user stories and acceptance criteria
- Define "done" precisely
- Create validation checklists
- Get stakeholder sign-off

**2. Automated Testing in CI/CD**
- Run tests on every commit
- Block merges on test failures
- Fast feedback loops
- Parallel test execution

**3. User Acceptance Testing (UAT)**
- Involve actual users when possible
- Test in realistic scenarios
- Collect qualitative feedback
- Validate assumptions

**4. Regular Code Reviews & Inspections**
- Review all code before merge
- Check for test coverage
- Validate against requirements
- Pair programming for complex features

**5. Prototyping & Mockups**
- Validate ideas before full implementation
- Get early feedback
- Iterate cheaply
- Reduce rework

**6. Configuration Management**
- Version control everything
- Reproducible builds
- Environment parity (dev/staging/prod)
- Dependency pinning

**7. Risk Assessment**
- Identify high-risk areas
- Prioritize testing efforts
- Plan mitigation strategies
- Monitor risk indicators

### Test Data Management

**Golden Datasets:**
- Capture real production-like data
- Version control or document generation method
- Refresh periodically
- Anonymize sensitive data

**Fixtures:**
- Small, focused test data
- Stored in version control
- Easy to understand
- Cover common scenarios

**Checkpoint-Based Testing:**
- Capture expensive API responses
- Reuse for fast, consistent tests
- Version control checkpoints
- Document data provenance

